# The application: the compile-worker. Each role's initramfs provides its own
# binary under this one name, so PID 1 needs no configuration and there is
# nothing to mis-set.
#
# Started once the clock is synchronised (see `timesync`), and stopped if the
# keeper ends. When it ends — cleanly or not — the guest ends with it (see
# `boot`).
#
# It runs as root, so the files it opens, all of them root's, stay its own; but
# `noroot` keeps uid 0 from bringing root's capabilities into the exec, and
# `-locked` keeps that so for anything it might start. It holds what
# `capabilities` raises with `^`, and nothing in the image carries file
# capabilities to add to it. Its supervisor (crates/engine-supervisor) holds
# four, all for the disposable child each compile runs in, and gives every one
# up in the child before its exec:
#   cap_setuid, cap_setgid  the child's identity, never reused, set between
#                           fork and exec;
#   cap_sys_resource        the child's `oom_score_adj`, written by a holder of
#                           it, is the floor the child cannot lower itself below;
#   cap_kill                killing a child, by then of another identity.
# It mounts nothing: its `proc` service mounts /proc as the supervisor needs it.
#
# Its stdin, stdout and stderr are the console, as a process PID 1 starts has
# always had here: `console=null` on a production command line discards them,
# and the `debug` one puts a real console on the serial port. What may reach the
# host goes to the log device by name (crates/safe-logger), never through these.
type = process
command = /bin/app
depends-on: timesync
restart = false
securebits = noroot noroot-locked
capabilities = ^cap_setuid,^cap_setgid,^cap_sys_resource,^cap_kill
options: shares-console
